--TEST--
A method reference naming a method the interface does not declare is refused where it is written — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0079 § 11's compile-checked reference, seen from the side that makes it
// worth having. `Mailer::purge` is the name of no method `Mailer` declares, so
// the assertion never compiles — which is the whole reason the member takes
// `Mailer::send` rather than `"send"`: renaming a method breaks the test that
// asserts about it instead of leaving it passing against a method that no
// longer exists.
//
// The spelling is admitted at this argument and nowhere else. `Mailer::purge`
// written anywhere a registry row did not ask for a method reference is the
// undefined class constant it reads as, and stays one.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool;
}

Mailer $mailer = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to): bool => true,
});
Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($mailer, Mailer::purge);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0829]: `Mailer` declares no method named `purge`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
